  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 345 AM EDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nBeginning Tuesday, the upper ridge becomes more positively tilted, \nthe center of the upper high sits centered near the Tennessee \nValley, and with surface winds veering to the southwest with the \naforementioned surface high making it to New England, WAA pushes \nhighs back into the 90s. Dew points reach back to the low to mid \n70s, driving the heat index to the low 100s over western parts of \nthe CWA. Little changes heading into Wednesday as the upper high \ndrives farther north over the CWA, keeping elevated heat indices in \nplace. Because of this and with communication with surrounding \noffices, the heat advisory has been extended into Wednesday. \n\nOver the last couple of days the trend has been day by day pushing \nthe upper high farther north, pushing first zonal flow and then a \nsecond upper ridge on Thursday farther north as well. In this upper \nflow, a west to east oriented front is expected to produce shower \nactivity, and with the system's northward trend, it should help to \nkeep most of the activity north of the CWA. The best chance for \nadditional rain will come late in the week as a cold front could sag \nsouth into the Lower Ohio Valley either Thursday night or during the \nday Friday, but with a upper low over the Gulf pushing west, south \nof the high, it may help to keep the high over the Lower Ohio Valley \nlonger, keeping us drier.",
